/* book Worked example: Q: Over 0 ≤ t ≤ 1 ms, s1(t) = 1 V for the whole interval, s2(t) = 2 V for the first half and 0 for the second, and s3(t) = 0 for the first half and 2 V for the second. Work in V·√ms. (a) Build the basis by Gram-Schmidt in the order s1, s2, s3. (b) Give the coordinates and energies. (c) Find the three distances. (d) Check Cauchy-Schwarz for s1, s2.
   1. Each half lasts 0.5 ms. E1 = 1² × 1 = 1 V²·ms, so ‖s1‖ = 1 V·√ms and φ1(t) = s1/‖s1‖ = 1/√ms over the whole interval.
   2. s2: c1 = ⟨s2, φ1⟩ = 2 × 1 × 0.5 + 0 = 1 V·√ms. Residual g2 = s2 − 1·φ1 = +1 V on the first half and −1 V on the second.
   3. ‖g2‖² = 1² × 0.5 + (−1)² × 0.5 = 1, so ‖g2‖ = 1 and φ2(t) = +1/√ms on the first half, −1/√ms on the second.
   4. s3: ⟨s3, φ1⟩ = 2 × 0.5 = 1; ⟨s3, φ2⟩ = 0 × 0.5 + 2 × (−1) × 0.5 = −1. Residual g3 = s3 − φ1 + φ2 = (0 − 1 + 1, 2 − 1 − 1) = 0 on both halves. No new axis: s3 = 2s1 − s2, so N = 2 for M = 3.
   5. Coordinates: s1 = (1, 0), s2 = (1, 1), s3 = (1, −1) V·√ms.
   6. Energies from coordinates: E1 = 1, E2 = 1 + 1 = 2, E3 = 2 V²·ms. Check from the waveform: E2 = 2² × 0.5 = 2.
   7. Distances: d12 = √(0² + 1²) = 1, d13 = 1, d23 = √(0² + 2²) = 2 V·√ms. Check d23 from the waveforms: ∫(s2 − s3)² = 2² × 0.5 + (−2)² × 0.5 = 4, so d23 = 2.
   8. Cauchy-Schwarz: ⟨s1, s2⟩ = 1 × 1 + 0 × 1 = 1 V²·ms, while ‖s1‖‖s2‖ = 1 × √2 = 1.414. 1 ≤ 1.414; not equal, because s2 is not a multiple of s1.
   Answer: Two axes, φ1 = 1/√ms flat and φ2 = ±1/√ms on the two halves; s1 = (1, 0), s2 = (1, 1), s3 = (1, −1) V·√ms; energies 1, 2, 2 V²·ms; distances 1, 1 and 2 V·√ms. */
