/* book exam traps: Assuming N = M. A signal that is a combination of earlier ones gives a zero residual and adds no axis; in the worked example M = 3 but N = 2. | Using the residual gm as an axis without dividing by ‖gm‖. Every later coordinate is then wrong. | Taking distance as a difference of energies. d² = Σ(smi − sni)²; s2 and s3 in the example have equal energy but are 2 V·√ms apart. | Expecting the coordinates to stay the same in another order. Start with s2 and its point becomes (√2, 0); only energies and distances are fixed. | Giving answers without units, or giving an axis in volts. Coordinates are in V·√s, energies in V²·s and axis heights in 1/√s. */
